# Suppresses a known, third-party-only ThreadSanitizer race inside OpenSSL's
# own X509_NAME_cmp/X509_cmp machinery (an ASN1_STRING read racing a
# concurrent write during certificate verification's canonical-encoding
# lazy-cache population), triggered when several concurrent client
# handshakes verify certificates against one shared ctls_ctx_t trust store
# (e.g. chttpclient's Tier 2/3 async engine firing several requests to
# certificate-verifying HTTPS origins at once). See ctls_conn_handshake_
# step's own doc comment in include/ctls.h for the full account: confirmed
# via an independent, ctls.c-free reproduction (raw OpenSSL, no c_collections
# code at all) to be entirely internal to libcrypto, with real precedent in
# OpenSSL's own issue tracker across multiple versions. Every concurrent
# handshake still completes correctly regardless (this is a data race the
# detector flags, not an actual verification/handshake failure), confirmed
# by a clean `make memtest` (valgrind) pass on the exact same test.
# Deliberately not "fixed" by locking around the verification or handshake
# step instead of suppressing the report: the only locking scope found to
# be 100% reliable (serialising the entire handshake call, not just
# verification) requires holding a lock across real network I/O waits
# between this module's own non-blocking handshake-step calls, which would
# serialise concurrent connections' actual data transfer, not just a brief
# CPU-bound moment; a real throughput regression to paper over what is
# very likely someone else's bug, not a fix.
#
# Anchored on _async_tls_advance (this module's own handshake-stepping call
# site, always symbol-resolved) rather than the OpenSSL-internal frame names
# directly, since the exact internal function touched varies across
# reproductions: some show ASN1_STRING_set/_cmp by name, others show only an
# unresolved libcrypto.so.3 offset a few frames deeper (same underlying
# category, confirmed via the same independent reproduction).
race:_async_tls_advance
